<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Reinhard Keiser</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1674">1674</a>–<a href="/years/1739">1739</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Reinhard Keiser (1674–1739) was a German composer who worked for most of his career at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oper-am-gansemarkt">Oper am Gänsemarkt</a>, which he also directed. He wrote more than sixty operas, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croesus">Croesus</a> and Octavia, and was the leading figure of early German opera. Handel and Telemann were among the composers influenced by his work.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/reinhard-keiser.jpg" alt="Reinhard Keiser" width="320" height="421" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Keiser, Reinhard; Hoë, Johann Joachim; Königsmarck, Maria Aurora · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Reinhard_Keiser_-_Die_gro%C3%9Fm%C3%BCtige_Tomyris_-_titlepage_of_the_libretto_from_1717.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Reinhard Keiser was born on 9 January 1674 in Teuchern, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony-anhalt">Saxony-Anhalt</a>, the son of the organist and teacher Gottfried Keiser. He was taught by other organists in the town and from the age of eleven at the Thomasschule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a>, where his teachers included Johann Schelle and Johann Kuhnau. In 1694 he became court composer to the Duke of Brunswick-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolfenbuttel">Wolfenbüttel</a>, whose court he had probably joined as early as 1692 to study its operas.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>About 1697 Keiser settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a> and became chief composer of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oper-am-gansemarkt">Oper am Gänsemarkt</a>, which he directed at times from 1702, his tenure often broken by political unrest. From 1703 to 1709 he turned the house from a public institution into a commercial venture with two or three performances a week. He introduced more varied arias and helped carry opera from the middle to the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a>. Early in 1704 the Hamburg season ended unexpectedly, and he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weissenfels">Weissenfels</a>, where his Almira was performed; he then answered Handel's Hamburg operas <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nero">Nero</a> and, later, Florindo and Daphne with works of his own, and in 1712 wrote his own passion music.</p><p>With the Hamburg Opera defunct in 1718, he left to seek work in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thuringia">Thuringia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stuttgart">Stuttgart</a>. Between 1721 and 1727 he travelled back and forth between Hamburg and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a>, receiving the title of Master of the Danish Royal Chapel, perhaps because <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georg-philipp-telemann">Georg Philipp Telemann</a> had been engaged in Hamburg in his absence. Relations between the two stayed good, and Telemann staged several of Keiser's operas.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1728 Keiser succeeded Johann Mattheson as precentor of St. Mary's Cathedral in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a>, and he wrote mostly church music there until his death on 12 September 1739. In an obituary Mattheson praised him above every other opera composer, and in 1745 Johann Adolf Scheibe ranked him with Kuhnau, Handel and Telemann, but his work was then largely forgotten for many decades. Besides his operas he wrote oratorios, among them a Brockes Passion of 1712.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Reinhard Keiser” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Oper am Gänsemarkt, opened in 1678, was the first public opera house in Germany, where <b>Reinhard Keiser</b> and the young George Frideric Handel worked; Georg Philipp Telemann was the city's director of music from 1721 to 1767, followed by Carl Philipp Emanuel Bach.</blockquote><a href="/empire/free-and-hanseatic-city-of-hamburg">Free and Hanseatic City of Hamburg · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Teuchern, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamburg">Hamburg</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.12,12.02&amp;year=1716" aria-label="Teuchern on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4823 392 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Reinhard Keiser"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5273 662h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5273 662h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.12,12.02&amp;year=1716">What was here in 1716? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
